y. Our own notes say B has relatively weak memory for numbers. That's a fact. So we already have that. So we don't need to ask. But we might want to ask about crew cooperation for B, but we have note: B makes nasty remarks about his colleagues. That's a concern. So we have that.\n\nWe need to decide our stance. Perhaps we can argue that while B is strong on reliability and stress, his communication issues and weak memory for numbers are serious for long-distance flights where numerical data (fuel, altitude) is critical. Candidate C has strong situational awareness and leadership, but concerns about being a show-off and not open to new ideas may affect crew cooperation. Candidate D has strong problem solving and responsibility but hot temper and know-it-all attitude may affect teamwork. Candidate A has quick decision making and calmness, but egocentric and poor communication.\n\nWe could argue that Candidate C's ability to anticipate dangerous situations and see complex connections is crucial for safety, and his leadership may outweigh the show-off perception, which could be mitigated. Or we could argue that B's weaknesses are too severe.\n\nWe need to make a focused contribution. Perhaps we can challenge the leading candidate B by highlighting the weak memory for numbers and nasty remarks, and propose that Candidate C might be a better fit due to situational awareness and leadership, despite the show-off perception.\n\nWe need to reference the discussion: Agent 3 recommended B. We'll say: \"I see the strengths of B, but I'm concerned about his weak memory for numbers and his nasty remarks, which could compromise attention and crew cooperation. In contrast, Candidate C demonstrates strong anticipatory skills and leadership, which are vital for long-distance flights.\" Provide evidence: C anticipates dangerous situations, sees complex connections, excellent spatial vision, very good leadership qualities. Concern: regarded as a show-off, not open to new ideas. Then we 